-- gleam.toml
target = "erlang"
-- src/app.gleam
import gleam/int

pub fn piped(count: Int) -> Int {
  count |> echo
}

pub fn piped_on_with_a_message(count: Int) -> String {
  count |> echo as "count" |> int.to_string
}
-- output
warning[grice_rules/no-echo]: Leftover `echo`
  ┌─ src/app.gleam:4:12
  │
4 │   count |> echo
  │            ^^^^ This prints debug output when it runs

`echo` is for debugging. `gleam build` accepts it, but `gleam publish`
refuses a package that still has one, and an application prints it whenever
this code runs.

Hint: Remove the `echo` and keep its value: write `x` for `echo x`, and
drop an `echo` step from a pipeline. If this module prints on purpose, use
`gleam/io` instead.

note[grice/untyped-definition]: Can't infer the types of this definition
  ┌─ src/app.gleam:7:1
  │
7 │ pub fn piped_on_with_a_message(count: Int) -> String {
  │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ girard couldn't type this definition

girard couldn't type `piped_on_with_a_message`: unsupported: an operator
after an `as` message. Typed rules get no answers about the code inside it,
so they may miss problems there.

Hint: If `gleam build` accepts this code, it's a gap in girard, the type
inference grice uses, so please report it at
https://github.com/alvivi/girard/issues.

